{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N4AEmaTMmfK4U4WXzRiHzf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Two tests failed. The issue is with the \"-\" (dash) argument. When \"-\" is passed as an explicit file argument, GNU wc (`gwc`) prints \"-\" as the filename:\n\n```\nref stdout:  \"      1       1       6 -\\n\"\ngo  stdout:  \"      1       1       6\\n\"\n```\n\nBut our code treats \"-\" as stdin and sets the name to \"\" (empty), which means no filename is printed. GNU wc prints \"-\" as the filename when \"-\" is explicitly passed as an argument.\n\n\nThe key insight is that I need to differentiate between \"-\" when it's explicitly provided by the user versus when it's implicitly added as the default stdin source. When \"-\" is an explicit argument, it should display as the filename, but when it's the implicit default, no filename should appear.\n\nI'm considering using a sentinel value approach—replacing the implicit \"-\" with an empty string \"\" to signal \"read stdin without showing a filename,\" while keeping \"-\" for explicit arguments that should display the filename. I'm realizing the core issue: when the user explicitly passes \"-\", it should display as the filename, but when we default to stdin with no arguments, we shouldn't show any filename. The cleanest solution is to use a different internal sentinel for implicit stdin instead of reusing \"-\". I see the issue now—when `-` is passed explicitly, it should print `-` as the filename like GNU wc does, but my current approach would print nothing instead. The fix is to keep `-` as-is for explicit stdin while using an empty string only for implicit stdin. I need to verify that `computeWidth` handles both cases correctly: it skips `-` explicitly, and the empty string would fail `os.Stat()` anyway, so it gets skipped too.\n\nLet me implement this fix now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"